\chapter{Logistic regression I: the basics}

\section{Probability, odds, and log-odds}

Logistic regression is built on a chain of three ways to express the same thing.
Get comfortable with the chain and the rest is easy.

\[
\underbrace{p}_{\text{probability}} \;\longrightarrow\;
\underbrace{\frac{p}{1-p}}_{\text{odds}} \;\longrightarrow\;
\underbrace{\log\!\left(\frac{p}{1-p}\right)}_{\text{log-odds, or logit}}
\]

\begin{plainenglish}[Odds in one sentence]
The odds is how many times more likely the event is than the non-event: a
probability of $0.75$ means the event is three times more likely than not, so the
odds are $3$, often written ``3 to 1''.
\end{plainenglish}

<<odds-table>>=
p      <- c(0.01, 0.10, 0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 0.90, 0.99)
odds   <- p / (1 - p)
logodds <- log(odds)
data.frame(probability = p, odds = round(odds, 3), log_odds = round(logodds, 3))
@

Look at what each column does at the extremes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Probability} is trapped between 0 and 1. Useless as a regression outcome.
  \item \textbf{Odds} runs from 0 to $\infty$. Better, but still one-sided, and
        it is not symmetric: doubling from $0.4$ to $0.8$ in probability is a very
        different jump from $0.1$ to $0.2$.
  \item \textbf{Log-odds} runs from $-\infty$ to $+\infty$ and is symmetric around
        zero. $p = 0.5$ gives log-odds $0$. Perfect for a linear predictor.
\end{itemize}

<<logit-shape, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.4, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: the logit transformation stretches probabilities near 0 and 1 out towards infinity. Right: the inverse, which is what the fitted model uses to turn a linear predictor back into a probability.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
pp <- seq(0.001, 0.999, length.out = 500)
plot(pp, log(pp/(1-pp)), type = "l", lwd = 3, col = PAL["primary"],
     xlab = "probability p", ylab = "logit(p)",
     main = "logit: (0,1) stretched to the whole line")
grid_book(); abline(h = 0, v = 0.5, lty = 3, col = PAL["accent"])

plot(pp, log(pp/(1-pp)), type = "n", xlab = "logit(p)", ylab = "probability p",
     xlim = c(-6, 6), ylim = c(0, 1), main = "and back again")
grid_book()
lines(seq(-6, 6, length.out = 400), plogis(seq(-6, 6, length.out = 400)),
      lwd = 3, col = PAL["green"])
abline(h = 0.5, v = 0, lty = 3, col = PAL["accent"])
@

\section{The model}

For a binary outcome $y_i \in \{0,1\}$ with $\Pr(y_i = 1) = p_i$:
\[
y_i \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_i), \qquad
\log\!\left(\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{1i} + \dots + \beta_p x_{pi}.
\]
Equivalently, solving for $p_i$:
\[
p_i = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\eta_i}} = \frac{e^{\eta_i}}{1 + e^{\eta_i}}.
\]

\section{A worked example: low birth weight}

The \fn{birthwt} data records 189 births at a US hospital. The outcome \fn{low} is
1 if the baby weighed under 2.5\,kg. We will model it from the mother's
characteristics.

<<bw-setup>>=
data(birthwt, package = "MASS")
bw <- within(birthwt, {
  race  <- factor(race, levels = 1:3, labels = c("white", "black", "other"))
  smoke <- factor(smoke, levels = 0:1, labels = c("no", "yes"))
  ht    <- factor(ht,    levels = 0:1, labels = c("no", "yes"))
  ui    <- factor(ui,    levels = 0:1, labels = c("no", "yes"))
})
str(bw[, c("low", "age", "lwt", "race", "smoke", "ht", "ui")])
table(bw$low)
@

So \Sexpr{sum(bw$low)} of \Sexpr{nrow(bw)} babies (\Sexpr{round(100*mean(bw$low),1)}\%)
were low birth weight. Always look at this before modelling: if the outcome is very
rare, you will need a lot of data to estimate anything precisely.

\begin{rulebox}[Events per variable]
A common guideline is at least 10 events \emph{and} 10 non-events for each
coefficient you estimate. With \Sexpr{sum(bw$low)} events here, models with more
than about 5 predictors start to be optimistic. This guideline is soft --- more
recent work suggests it can be relaxed or tightened depending on the signal --- but
it stops you from fitting 15 predictors to 30 events.
\end{rulebox}

\subsection{Fitting}

<<bw-fit>>=
fit1 <- glm(low ~ age + lwt + race + smoke + ht + ui,
            family = binomial(link = "logit"), data = bw)
summary(fit1)
@

\subsection{Reading the coefficients}

Everything in the \fn{Estimate} column is a change in \emph{log-odds}. Nobody
thinks in log-odds. Exponentiate:

<<bw-or>>=
or <- exp(cbind(OR = coef(fit1), confint(fit1)))
round(or, 3)
@

Now read them as multipliers of the odds:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \fn{smokeyes}: OR $=$ \Sexpr{round(exp(coef(fit1))["smokeyes"], 2)}. Mothers
        who smoked had about \Sexpr{round(exp(coef(fit1))["smokeyes"], 1)} times the
        odds of a low-birth-weight baby, holding the other variables fixed.
  \item \fn{lwt}: OR $=$ \Sexpr{round(exp(coef(fit1))["lwt"], 3)}, i.e.\ each extra
        pound of the mother's weight multiplies the odds by
        \Sexpr{round(exp(coef(fit1))["lwt"], 3)} --- a
        \Sexpr{abs(round((exp(coef(fit1))["lwt"] - 1) * 100, 1))}\% \emph{decrease}
        per pound. Small per pound, but over 30 pounds it compounds to a factor of
        \Sexpr{round(exp(30 * coef(fit1)["lwt"]), 2)}.
  \item \fn{raceblack} and \fn{raceother}: these are compared with \fn{white},
        because \fn{white} is the first level of the factor and \R{} makes the
        first level the reference.
\end{itemize}

\begin{keyidea}[The three-part sentence for an odds ratio]
``Holding the other variables constant, a \textit{one-unit increase in X} is
associated with the odds of \textit{Y} being multiplied by \textit{exp(beta)},
95\% CI \textit{[lower, upper]}.'' If you write every result in that shape you
will never misreport a logistic regression.
\end{keyidea}

\subsection{Rescaling to make a coefficient readable}

An odds ratio ``per pound'' is awkward. Rescale the variable to a meaningful unit
before fitting, or multiply the coefficient afterwards:

<<bw-rescale>>=
## Per 10 pounds instead of per pound
exp(10 * coef(fit1)["lwt"])
## Or refit with a rescaled variable -- same thing, easier to report
fit1b <- update(fit1, . ~ . - lwt + I(lwt / 10))
round(exp(cbind(OR = coef(fit1b), confint.default(fit1b))), 3)["I(lwt/10)", ]
@

\begin{warningbox}[Odds ratios are not risk ratios]
An odds ratio of 3 does \emph{not} mean the event is three times as likely. When
the outcome is rare (say under 10\%) the two are close, but when it is common they
diverge badly. If the baseline probability is 0.4, an OR of 3 corresponds to a
probability of 0.67 --- a risk ratio of only 1.7, not 3. Say ``odds'' when you mean
odds.
\end{warningbox}

<<or-vs-rr, fig.width=7, fig.height=3.6, out.width="0.8\\textwidth", fig.cap="How far an odds ratio is from the corresponding risk ratio, as the baseline probability rises. They only agree for rare outcomes.">>=
par_book()
p0 <- seq(0.005, 0.6, length.out = 300)
for (OR in c(1.5, 2, 3, 5)) {
  odds1 <- OR * p0 / (1 - p0)
  p1 <- odds1 / (1 + odds1)
  rr <- p1 / p0
  if (OR == 1.5) {
    plot(p0, rr, type = "l", lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["primary"], ylim = c(1, 5.2),
         xlab = "baseline probability", ylab = "corresponding risk ratio",
         main = "An odds ratio overstates the risk ratio")
    grid_book()
  } else {
    lines(p0, rr, lwd = 2.5,
          col = c(PAL["accent"], PAL["green"], PAL["red"])[which(c(2,3,5) == OR)])
  }
  abline(h = OR, lty = 3, col = PAL["grey"])
}
legend("topright", bty = "n", cex = 0.8, lwd = 2.5,
       col = c(PAL["primary"], PAL["accent"], PAL["green"], PAL["red"]),
       legend = paste("OR =", c(1.5, 2, 3, 5)))
@

\section{Predicted probabilities}

Coefficients answer ``how much does this change things?''. Predicted probabilities
answer ``what actually happens to a specific person?'' --- and are usually far more
persuasive to a non-statistical audience.

<<bw-predict>>=
newdat <- expand.grid(
  age   = 25,
  lwt   = seq(80, 250, by = 5),
  race  = "white",
  smoke = c("no", "yes"),
  ht    = "no",
  ui    = "no"
)
pr <- predict(fit1, newdata = newdat, type = "link", se.fit = TRUE)
newdat$fit <- plogis(pr$fit)
newdat$lo  <- plogis(pr$fit - 1.96 * pr$se.fit)
newdat$hi  <- plogis(pr$fit + 1.96 * pr$se.fit)
head(newdat[, c("lwt", "smoke", "fit", "lo", "hi")], 4)
@

\begin{keyidea}[Always build intervals on the link scale]
Compute the confidence interval for $\eta$ (where the normal approximation is
good), \emph{then} push both endpoints through \fn{plogis}. If you build the
interval directly on the probability scale you can get limits below 0 or above 1.
\end{keyidea}

<<bw-predict-plot, fig.width=7.5, fig.height=4.2, out.width="0.85\\textwidth", fig.cap="Fitted probability of low birth weight against the mother's weight, for smokers and non-smokers, with 95\\% confidence bands. This is the plot to put in a report.">>=
par_book()
sm <- subset(newdat, smoke == "yes"); ns <- subset(newdat, smoke == "no")
plot(newdat$lwt, newdat$fit, type = "n", ylim = c(0, 0.85),
     xlab = "mother's weight at last period (lbs)",
     ylab = "P(low birth weight)",
     main = "Fitted probabilities, 25-year-old white mother, no HT/UI")
grid_book()
polygon(c(sm$lwt, rev(sm$lwt)), c(sm$lo, rev(sm$hi)),
        col = adjustcolor(PAL["red"], 0.15), border = NA)
polygon(c(ns$lwt, rev(ns$lwt)), c(ns$lo, rev(ns$hi)),
        col = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.15), border = NA)
lines(sm$lwt, sm$fit, lwd = 3, col = PAL["red"])
lines(ns$lwt, ns$fit, lwd = 3, col = PAL["primary"])
legend("topright", bty = "n", lwd = 3, col = c(PAL["red"], PAL["primary"]),
       legend = c("smoker", "non-smoker"), cex = 0.85)
rug(bw$lwt, col = adjustcolor(PAL["grey"], 0.6))
@

The \fn{rug} at the bottom shows where the data actually are. Notice the
confidence band widens where there are few mothers --- above about 200 pounds the
model is extrapolating and you should not trust it.

\section{Categorical predictors and reference levels}

\R{} turns a factor with $k$ levels into $k-1$ dummy variables, all compared with
the reference level. You control which level is the reference:

<<relevel>>=
levels(bw$race)                        # reference is the first level
bw$race2 <- relevel(bw$race, ref = "other")
fit_rl <- glm(low ~ smoke + race2, family = binomial, data = bw)
round(exp(coef(fit_rl)), 3)
@

To test a factor \emph{as a whole} (all its levels at once) you need a single test
with the right degrees of freedom, not a glance at the individual $p$-values:

<<anova-factor>>=
anova(fit1, test = "LRT")
@

That table adds terms one at a time in the order you wrote them and gives a
likelihood-ratio test for each. \fn{race} has 2 degrees of freedom because it has
three levels. Chapter 13 goes into these tests properly.

\begin{warningbox}[Order matters in \fn{anova()}]
\fn{anova.glm} produces \emph{sequential} (Type I) tests. Change the order of terms
in the formula and the numbers change. For order-independent tests use
\fn{car::Anova(fit1, type = "II")}.
\end{warningbox}

<<car-anova>>=
car::Anova(fit1, type = "II", test.statistic = "LR")
@

\section{Interactions on the logit scale}

An interaction means the effect of one variable depends on another. On the logit
scale it is a ratio of odds ratios.

<<interaction>>=
fit_int <- glm(low ~ smoke * ht + lwt, family = binomial, data = bw)
round(exp(coef(fit_int)), 3)
@

Because the model is multiplicative, the odds ratio for smoking among mothers
\emph{with} hypertension is the product of the main effect and the interaction
term. It is far safer to let \pkg{emmeans} do this arithmetic:

<<emmeans-int>>=
library(emmeans)
emm <- emmeans(fit_int, ~ smoke | ht, type = "response")
pairs(emm, reverse = TRUE)
@

\begin{plainenglish}[What that table says]
It gives the smoking odds ratio separately within each hypertension group, with
the standard errors worked out correctly. Doing this by hand from the coefficient
table is possible but easy to get wrong, especially with factors of more than two
levels.
\end{plainenglish}

\begin{warningbox}[Interactions are hungry]
An interaction between two binary variables splits your events into four cells.
With \Sexpr{sum(bw$low)} events, a smoke $\times$ hypertension interaction is
estimated from very few observations --- look at the standard error above. Wide
intervals here are honest, not a failure.
\end{warningbox}
